{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Babel for supporting PBS. \nThe universe thrums with quantum fields;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the particles of matter and force \nemerge as vibrational manifestations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the deep symmetries of these fields. The \nlayers and reflections of those symmetries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16560,
    "dDurationMs": 13120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give us the wonderful richness of what we \ncall the standard model of particle physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've got a couple of quick announcements \nbefore we start. First, our eternal battle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against the algorithm continues. The best way to \nencourage YouTube to share our videos is to like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and comment. Doing both really makes a difference. \nAnd if you're new here, subscribe, hit the bell,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and introduce yourself in the comments. We're \nfriendly. We've got our first UV glow merch of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2026. The Kerr rotating black hole tea and hoodie \nare UV charged, so after some time in the sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it starts to glow. Perfect for showing love \nfor the place where light can't escape. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you want to make sure your ideas don't escape \neither, pick up our black hole rotation notebook.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UV shirts are limited time only and links \nare in the description. Now onto the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Three generations each of quarks and leptons \nwith each generation bisected via isospin. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the leptons, that splitting gives us the massive \nelectrons, muons and taus and their corresponding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultra-light neutrino counterparts. That’s the \nsimplest representation of the standard model,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but all of these are split again into matter \nand antimatter. And there’s one more lesser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100880,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known reflection—chirality. This is a sort \nof fundamental handedness or spin relative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their direction of motion. Left- and right-handed \nquarks, left- and right-handed electrons, muons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113040,
    "dDurationMs": 9520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and taus. But, weirdly, here cosmic symmetry seems \nto fail: there are only left-handed neutrinos. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No right-handed neutrino has ever been \ndetected, which seems odd at first but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may not be so surprising. Regular, left-handed \nneutrinos are famously very difficult to detect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because they only interact via gravity and \nthe weak nuclear force—both of which are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notably weak. The weak force, being 10^24 \ntimes stronger than gravity, is the only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way we can actually see individual neutrinos.\nHowever, the weak force is also victim to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broken cosmic symmetries: it only affects \nparticles with left-handed chirality.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right-handed particles simply don’t feel \nit. So, lacking any other type of charge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the right-handed neutrino has no way to interact \nwith any of the forces of nature besides through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169920,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its negligible gravity. This is why we call the \nhypothetical particle the sterile neutrino—it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "barren of any capacity for interaction by the \nquantum forces. If regular neutrinos are difficult  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184640,
    "dDurationMs": 10320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to detect, sterile ones seem to be impossible.\nSo are these gaps in the standard model missing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because we haven’t detected the \nparticle? But there are some major  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problems in physics that could be solved \nvery neatly if sterile neutrinos do exist. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s for this reason that physicists have been \nhunting for the sterile neutrino for decades,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and why were getting excited as subtle \nevidence of the sterile neutrino started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to grow. And it’s for this reason that \nthe brand new results from the Fermilab  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221360,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MicroBOONE experiment are so shocking. Our most \nadvanced experiment yet suggests that this hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the standard model really is empty and that \nthe sterile neutrino doesn’t exist at all. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with why the right-handed neutrino \nwas really left out of the standard model in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240720,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first place. When Weinberg, Salam, and Glashow put \nthe finishing touches on the standard model, they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247920,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were as economical as possible. They only included \nthe particles needed to explain our observations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The chiral reflections of the quarks and \nthe heavy leptons were non-negotiable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "largely because these are needed to give \nthese families of particles mass by granting a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266800,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connection to the Higgs field. But in the 1960s we \ndidn’t know whether the neutrino had mass—in fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weinberg et al. assumed that it did not. Not \nonly was the right-handed neutrino not needed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but including them generated problems with \ncertain conservation laws—lepton flavor to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be precise. Cleaner to just leave them out.\nIt wasn’t until the 1990s that we discovered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that neutrinos have mass after all. We \ndiscovered the neutrinos spontaneously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oscillate between their three flavours—electron, \nmuon and tau. That can only be true if they have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304320,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass. As it turns out, absurdly tiny masses.\nWe don’t know how neutrinos get their masses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we just eliminated one of the main reasons \nthat Weinberg et al. left sterile neutrinos out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard Model in the first place. They \ncould, in principle, do the same job as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323840,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chiral reflections of electrons, granting mass via \nthe Higgs mechanism. In fact, they can solve two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331200,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mysteries at once. The extraordinarily tiny mass \nof the neutrino warrants its own explanation. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338720,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sterile neutrinos exist, and if they’re actually \nextremely massive, then they could a) explain why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos have mass and b) drive down the mass \nof regular neutrinos through something called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350960,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see-saw process. Not something to get into today.\nThere’s another use of a hypothetical particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s both massive and impossible to detect \nby any means other than gravity. That’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exact requirement for the elusive dark \nmatter particle. So sterile neutrinos  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem tailor made to explain why 80% of \nthe matter in the universe is invisible. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378480,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so the search for the sterile neutrino began. \nBut how do we go about detecting the undetectable? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regular neutrinos are hard enough. They only \ninteract via the weak force, and the weak force  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a very short range. That means a neutrino \nhas to pass very close to an atomic nucleus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for anything to happen. For a mid-energy solar \nneutrino passing through the Earth, there’s only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403040,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a one in a billion chance it’ll be stopped by a \nnucleus. But when that does happen, its energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410240,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gets converted into a cascade of particles that, \nin principle, can be seen. So we can see neutrinos  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as we play the numbers right—enough \nneutrinos through a big enough detector. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about sterile neutrinos? If they don’t \neven interact by the weak force, what hope do we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have? Well, a sterile neutrino can’t exchange \nforce-carrying bosons with other particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437440,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it can interact with itself in a certain \nsense. In particular, it can participate in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrino oscillations that I mentioned earlier. \nIf electron, muon, and tau neutrinos can transform  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into each other, then they can also transform into \ntheir chiral-reflected counterparts—the sterile  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrino. And this is where we have a prospect \nfor “detecting” them—by looking for anomalies in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the oscillations of known neutrino flavours.\nLet’s look at the first effort to catch the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sterile neutrino as an example. This was at Los \nAlamos in the 90s with the Liquid Scintillator  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutrino Detector. The experiment goes like \nthis: a beam of muon neutrinos from a particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerator is directed into a vat of mineral oil. \nWhen a tiny fraction of those neutrinos collide  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a neutron, one possible reaction is for the \nneutron and muon-neutrino to be converted into a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492640,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proton and a muon. The muon zips off through the \noil at a near the vacuum speed of light. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the muon is moving faster than the diminished \nspeed of that actual light has in the oil. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leads to something like an optical sonic \nboom—what we call Cherenkov radiation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it’s that radiation that LSND detected.\nA similar reaction can happen if the incoming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle is an electron neutrino instead of \na muon neutrino. In that case it produces a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524880,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proton and an electron rather than a muon. That \nelectron will still produce Cherenkov radiation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but because the electron is much lighter than the \nmuon it decelerates quickly and in the process  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537280,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emits photons, which themselves can undergo pair \nproduction to become electron-positron pairs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which emit further Cherenkov photons, which \ninduce pair production, etc. with the process  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continuing until the energy dissipates. \nThis is called an electromagnetic cascade."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Cherenkov ring produced by an \nelectron and its messy EM cascade  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is fuzzier and fainter than the crisp, bright \nring produced by a muon. This is what allows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568960,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientists to distinguish whether the incoming \nneutrino was of the muon or electron flavour. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even though the LSND experiment used a \nmuon-neutrino beam, there are two ways for it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see events from electron neutrinos. One is that \nthere’s a very small contamination of electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos in the beam and from cosmic rays and \nthings like that. The other is that muon neutrinos  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595600,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can oscillate into electron neutrinos before \nreaching the tank. However, the LSND experiment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was set up so that the detector chamber was way \ntoo close to the beam source for the oscillations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to occur in significant numbers. Basically, \nit expected very few electron neutrinos.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that design is the key to it finding our \nhypothetical neutrino type—the sterile neutrino. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the sterile neutrino exists and has \na relatively low mass, it gives the muon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrino an intermediate step to oscillate into \nan electron neutrino. That massively increases the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance of that transition, enabling a good number \nof muon neutrinos to flip before reaching the LSND  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641200,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chamber. The outcome should be that LSND saw many \nmore electron-induced electromagnetic cascades. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649920,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it saw exactly that—enough to point to \nsterile neutrinos with a mass of around 1 eV,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which puts it at the low end of possible masses. \nThis was compelling enough to inspire follow-ups.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666560,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The MiniBooNE experiment at Fermilab, built in the \n2000s, was designed to test the emerging anomaly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with higher sensitivity. More neutrinos, a \nbigger tank of mineral oil, etc. In 2018,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MiniBooNE released its final data — and once \nagain, they saw an excess of fuzzy Cherenkov  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686560,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rings consistent with electron-muon events, and \nsupporting the 1 eV sterile muon hypothesis. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another very different type of experiment also \nsupported this. The Italian GALLEX and the Soviet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "SAGE experiments looked for conversion \nof gallium into germanium after impact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with electron neutrinos. Too few conversions were \nobserved in both experiments. One explanation was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that these electron neutrinos were depleted \nby converting into sterile neutrinos. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interesting thing here is that the sterile \nneutrino would also have to be around 1 eV. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This feels like it’s coming together in \nsupport of the sterile neutrino. But there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are two reasons not to colour-in the gaps in our \nstandard model table just yet. The first is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are contradictions to the sterile neutrino \nhypothesis. Other experiments similar in design  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to LSND and miniboone found no excess of electron \nneutrinos. And several experiments found another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747360,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contradiction—if muon-neutrinos are converting to \nsterile neutrinos, then there should be fewer muon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos. But that’s never been detected, not \nfrom accelerator experiments, not from nuclear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reactors, and even the neutrinos from the Sun \nas measured by the Ice Cube observatory at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "south pole are perfectly consistent with neutrinos \noscillating only between the three known types. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774480,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we have these different types of \nexperiments giving weirdly contradictory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results. Finding sterile neutrinos would be \nincredibly important, but it’s also important  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784960,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to figure out this discrepancy. MiniBoone \nneeded to be upgraded - to MicroBoone. That’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "short for Micro Booster Neutrino Experiment.\nIt was designed to eliminate the main source  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of uncertainty in its predecessor. So one way to \nexplain the electron excess in both miniBooNE and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805680,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LSND is that these fuzzy electron Cherenkov rings \nmay be due to something completely different. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813840,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutrino collisions often produce neutral pion \nparticles, which decay into pairs of gamma rays,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821600,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and those in turn can create electron-positron \npairs via pair production. And those particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829200,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then produce cones of Cherenkov radiation and \nelectromagnetic showers. If those showers overlap,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the resulting ring can look just like the \nsingle rings from a proper electron-neutrino  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event. MicroBooNE’s main job is to filter out \nthese false signals—we’ll call them photon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "events—from the actual electron-neutrino events.\nMicroBooNE sits just downstream of MiniBooNE  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the same neutrino beam at Fermilab. But it’s \nbuilt around a completely different technology — a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863040,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid argon time projection chamber. MiniBooNE \ndistinguished between muon-like or electron-like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "events based on the quality of the Cherenkov \nrings. But this new chamber type can actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "track the detailed trajectory of the particles \nproduced in the neutrino collision. This gives a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882400,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "foolproof way of sorting real electron-neutrino \nevents from the false ones I described. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when a neutrino hits an atomic nucleus, \nthere’s always a spray of various charged  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles like protons and pions from the \npoint of impact—the “vertex”--that allows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901360,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us to localize the collision point. If \nmuon is produced by a muon-neutrino,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its track will begin at the vertex and it’ll \ntravel a long distance in a straightish line. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914800,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If an electron is produced by an \nelectron-neutrino collision, it’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918800,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel a shorter distance and then create an \nelectromagnetic cascade like I already described."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925600,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that with miniBooNE these electron events \nwere hard to separate from photon events. In the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "latter case we have a neutrino impact and the \nresulting spray of particles, which includes two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gamma ray photons. These then travel through the \ntank leaving no track at all until they themselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convert into electron-positron pairs, which can \nthen start a visible electromagnetic cascade. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way to distinguish a genuine electron-neutrino \nevent from one of these false events is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957600,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s a clear gap between the collision \nvertex and the beginning of the EM cascade."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what did MicroBooNE find? Well, not \nthe sterile neutrino. It found no excess  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of electron-neutrino events that might \nindicate a sterile neutrino in the mix,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "once MicroBooNE eliminated the false signal from \nphoton events. The first MicroBooNE data release  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and results were published in 2021, and already \nit wasn’t looking good for the sterile neutrino.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at the end of last year—December 2025—the \nMicroBooNE collaboration published new results  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which a source of confusion was removed \nusing a second neutrino beam. The absence of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002160,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an electron-neutrino excess was confirmed. This \nfinal analysis was also able to confirm that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009520,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the apparent excess observed by MiniBooNE can \nbe completely accounted for by photon events,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it ruled out sterile neutrinos as the cause \nof the anomaly in the early gallium experiments. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why is this a big deal? The sterile neutrino \nis probably the simplest possible extension of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard model, and so the anomalies \nobserved in earlier experiments made its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existence very plausible. Likely, in the minds \nof some. MicroBooNE has basically eliminated any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that evidence for the sterile neutrino. \nThat sends the particle back to the realm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048800,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of pure speculation. We have no empirical \nreason whatsoever to believe it exists. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056480,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it may still exist. MicroBooNE is sensitive \nto a mass range of around .1 to 10 eV. But if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sterile neutrino has a much higher mass it \ncould still be out there. And that means the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle remains a solid prospect for explaining \nthe tinyness of the other neutrino masses and for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter. For those, the sterile neutrino \nwould need a many orders of magnitude larger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the sensitivity of MicroBooNE. In \nfact, the non-existence of light sterile  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos could even be seen as a boost for \nthis particle's role in those mysteries. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to detect massive sterile neutrinos \nwe need to try other things, and there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are various works in progress to look for \nthe subtle signatures of their existence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both in labs and out there in the universe. \nAnd these are topics for another time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the meantime, our congratulations to \nthe good folk at Fermilab for sorting out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this strange anomaly, and for their continuing \neffort in understanding the neutrino—the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mysterious particle in the Standard Model. If you \nwant to learn more about their neutrino program,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130160,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe watch that episode when Don Lincoln \nand Fermilab actually hosted PBS Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137200,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Babbel for supporting \nPBS. If you spend any time online,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you might have found that today's world is highly \ninternational. That's why Babel teaches you how to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have real world conversations. Babel's lessons are \ndeveloped by over 650 language experts to help you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start speaking a new language in a minimum of \n3 weeks. Lessons prepare you to have practical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conversations and guide learners towards real \nlife conversations. Here it is right here. Aprendo  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "espanol means I'm learning Spanish. So if you're \ninterested in learning a new language in this new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1172320,
    "dDurationMs": 20720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year, you can get up to 55% off your subscription \nby clicking the links in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
